        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. Boundary endpoints: No interval endpoint remains implicit in the Statement.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $X$ be a set of state labels. A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. The coefficient is a dimensionless formal amount; state labels retain their tags, so equal coefficients on different labels do not identify those labels. Compounds are equal exactly when their coefficient functions are equal.\n\nFor compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, define composition and nonnegative scaling pointwise by $(c+d)(x):=c(x)+d(x)$ and $(tc)(x):=t c(x)$. The zero function is the empty compound; in particular $0c=0$. Comparison axioms involving scaling still require a strictly positive scalar unless explicitly stated otherwise. The **mass** of $c$ is the finite sum $m(c):=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)$. It is dimensionless bookkeeping mass, not thermodynamic mass or energy. The compound is positive when $m(c)>0$.\n\nFor every state label $x\\in X$, the **unit-state compound** $e_x$ is the coefficient function with $e_x(y)=1$ if $y=x$ and $e_x(y)=0$ otherwise. Its support is $\\{x\\}$, so it is a tagged compound. In compound comparisons, a state label $x$ denotes $e_x$. Every compound has the canonical finite decomposition $c=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)e_x$, with an empty sum when $c=0$; coefficients on this support are strictly positive. The mass-one property $m(e_x)=1$ and the equivalence $m(c)=0\\Longleftrightarrow c=0$ are proved in [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nThe formula for mass is independent of the chosen finite set containing the support, and composition and scaling obey $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]] proves these assertions.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "705fae80d7c3349801911a729dc7453a2872df3c7d50a2cf1128b4eafc846239",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. Boundary nonempty-choice: No arbitrary choice is used; any existence argument is for the fixed finite or individual case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $X$ be a set of state labels. A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. The coefficient is a dimensionless formal amount; state labels retain their tags, so equal coefficients on different labels do not identify those labels. Compounds are equal exactly when their coefficient functions are equal.\n\nFor compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, define composition and nonnegative scaling pointwise by $(c+d)(x):=c(x)+d(x)$ and $(tc)(x):=t c(x)$. The zero function is the empty compound; in particular $0c=0$. Comparison axioms involving scaling still require a strictly positive scalar unless explicitly stated otherwise. The **mass** of $c$ is the finite sum $m(c):=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)$. It is dimensionless bookkeeping mass, not thermodynamic mass or energy. The compound is positive when $m(c)>0$.\n\nFor every state label $x\\in X$, the **unit-state compound** $e_x$ is the coefficient function with $e_x(y)=1$ if $y=x$ and $e_x(y)=0$ otherwise. Its support is $\\{x\\}$, so it is a tagged compound. In compound comparisons, a state label $x$ denotes $e_x$. Every compound has the canonical finite decomposition $c=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)e_x$, with an empty sum when $c=0$; coefficients on this support are strictly positive. The mass-one property $m(e_x)=1$ and the equivalence $m(c)=0\\Longleftrightarrow c=0$ are proved in [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nThe formula for mass is independent of the chosen finite set containing the support, and composition and scaling obey $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]] proves these assertions.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "705fae80d7c3349801911a729dc7453a2872df3c7d50a2cf1128b4eafc846239",
