        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. Boundary one: No one-element boundary changes the result beyond the stated hypotheses.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. These signed maps are formal comparison notation, not compounds. Put $d:=v-u$ and define $d^+(x):=\\max\\{d(x),0\\}$ and $d^-(x):=\\max\\{-d(x),0\\}$. Then $d^+,d^-$ are nonnegative finite-support compounds of equal mass, as verified in [[lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation]]. Define\n\n$$u\\preceq_s v\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d^-\\preceq d^+,$$\n\nusing the compound comparison relation of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]]. Thus positive coefficients of $v-u$ remain on the right and negative coefficients move to the left. Signed notation is never a claim that a negative amount of a physical system exists.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "1a4d351a8a1eb29b6af48b06e8ccfe40be8dd65c257e619b25d1b6ff7a195a19",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
