    "lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set of state labels. A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. The coefficient is a dimensionless formal amount; state labels retain their tags, so equal coefficients on different labels do not identify those labels. Compounds are equal exactly when their coefficient functions are equal.\n\nFor compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, define composition and nonnegative scaling pointwise by $(c+d)(x):=c(x)+d(x)$ and $(tc)(x):=t c(x)$. The zero function is the empty compound; in particular $0c=0$. Comparison axioms involving scaling still require a strictly positive scalar unless explicitly stated otherwise. The **mass** of $c$ is the finite sum $m(c):=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)$. It is dimensionless bookkeeping mass, not thermodynamic mass or energy. The compound is positive when $m(c)>0$.\n\nFor every state label $x\\in X$, the **unit-state compound** $e_x$ is the coefficient function with $e_x(y)=1$ if $y=x$ and $e_x(y)=0$ otherwise. Its support is $\\{x\\}$, so it is a tagged compound. In compound comparisons, a state label $x$ denotes $e_x$. Every compound has the canonical finite decomposition $c=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)e_x$, with an empty sum when $c=0$; coefficients on this support are strictly positive. The mass-one property $m(e_x)=1$ and the equivalence $m(c)=0\\Longleftrightarrow c=0$ are proved in [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nThe formula for mass is independent of the chosen finite set containing the support, and composition and scaling obey $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]] proves these assertions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every tagged scalable compound $c$, the mass in [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] is independent of the finite summation set used. For any compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; $m(0)=0$. For every $x\\in X$, the unit-state compound $e_x$ has $m(e_x)=1$. A compound has zero mass if and only if it is the empty compound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a nonempty set $X$ and the compounds of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. A **comparison relation** $\\preceq$ is defined only for pairs $(A,B)$ with $m(A)=m(B)$. Write $A\\sim B$ when both $A\\preceq B$ and $B\\preceq A$; this is equivalence, not equality of coefficient functions. The following are explicit hypotheses on $\\preceq$:\n\n1. **Preorder:** $A\\preceq A$, and $A\\preceq B\\preceq C$ implies $A\\preceq C$.\n2. **Composition consistency:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $A+C\\preceq B+C$ for every compound $C$.\n3. **Positive scaling:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $tA\\preceq tB$ for every real $t>0$.\n4. **Splitting and recombination:** for $x\\in X$, $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, the one-state compound $te_x$ is equivalent to $se_x+(t-s)e_x$.\n5. **Stability:** for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive compounds $Z_0,Z_1$ with $m(Z_0)=m(Z_1)$, and a sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to $0$, if $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\preceq B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\preceq B$.\n6. **Comparison hypothesis (CH):** for every equal-mass pair $A,B$, either $A\\preceq B$ or $B\\preceq A$ (or both).\n\nThese are mathematical assumptions, not conclusions about a physical system. No antisymmetry, continuity, convexity, or strict comparison is included. Strict comparison means $A\\preceq B$ and not $B\\preceq A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-signed-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. These signed maps are formal comparison notation, not compounds. Put $d:=v-u$ and define $d^+(x):=\\max\\{d(x),0\\}$ and $d^-(x):=\\max\\{-d(x),0\\}$. Then $d^+,d^-$ are nonnegative finite-support compounds of equal mass, as verified in [[lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation]]. Define\n\n$$u\\preceq_s v\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d^-\\preceq d^+,$$\n\nusing the compound comparison relation of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]]. Thus positive coefficients of $v-u$ remain on the right and negative coefficients move to the left. Signed notation is never a claim that a negative amount of a physical system exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-canonical-natural",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]) with additive identity $0_F$ and\nmultiplicative identity $1_F$. Define\n$\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to F$ by recursion on $\\mathbb{N}$\n([[def-natural-numbers]], [[thm-recursion]]):\n\n$$\\iota(0) \\;:=\\; 0_F, \\qquad \\iota(n+1) \\;:=\\; \\iota(n) + 1_F .$$\n\n$\\iota(n)$ is the **canonical natural** of $n$ in $F$. It is also written\n$n \\cdot 1_F$, and for $n \\ge 1$ it is $1_F$ added to itself $n$ times.\n\n**Why the notation is needed at all.** A natural number in this library is a von\nNeumann natural, that is a set ([[def-natural-numbers]]), and a set is not an\nelement of $F$. So $1/n$, $n^{\\alpha}$ and $x/n$ are not expressions of $F$ when\n$n$ is a natural: what they mean is $1/\\iota(n)$, $\\iota(n)^{\\alpha}$ and\n$x/\\iota(n)$. The map $\\iota$ is what carries a counting number into the field,\nand writing it is the whole reason a reader meets $1/\\iota(k+1)$ where an\ninformal text would write $1/(k+1)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-of-naturals-positive",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field, with field structure as in [[def-field]], and for\n$n \\ge 1$ let $n \\cdot 1_F$ denote the canonical natural number\n$\\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$, defined recursively by $1 \\cdot 1_F = 1_F$\nand $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F = n \\cdot 1_F + 1_F$. Then for every $n \\ge 1$:\n\n- $n \\cdot 1_F > 0$ and $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F > n \\cdot 1_F$;\n- the map $n \\mapsto n \\cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing, hence injective, on $\\{1, 2, 3, \\dots\\}$;\n- $(m+n) \\cdot 1_F = m \\cdot 1_F + n \\cdot 1_F$ and $(mn) \\cdot 1_F = (m \\cdot 1_F)(n \\cdot 1_F)$ for all $m, n \\ge 1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-reciprocal-naturals-tend-zero",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$, the sequence $\\epsilon_n:=1/(n+1)$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, is positive, strictly decreasing, and converges to zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For equal-total $u,v$, $d=v-u$ has finite support and total sum zero. Pointwise $d=d^+-d^-$, so finite-sum linearity gives $m(d^+)-m(d^-)=0$; both maps are finite-support and nonnegative. Thus the definition in [F2] compares equal-mass compounds. If $u,v$ are nonnegative, set $k(x)=\\min\\{u(x),v(x)\\}$; then $u=k+(v-u)^-$ and $v=k+(v-u)^+$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Suppose $A+C\\preceq B+C$ and $m(C)>0$. For each $k\\in\\mathbb N$, set $n=\\sigma(k)$ and $r=\\iota_{\\mathbb N}(n)>0$, and write $A/r=(1/r)A$. The embedding's successor rule and finite induction show that the sum of $n$ copies of $A/r$ is $r(A/r)=A$, and likewise for $B$. Scaling the given comparison by $1/r$ gives $A/r+C/r\\preceq B/r+C/r$. By composition consistency, replace the $n$ copies of $A/r$ one at a time by $B/r$, keeping the already replaced copies, the remaining copies, and $C/r$ as spectators; after these finitely many replacements, $A+\\epsilon_k C\\preceq B+\\epsilon_k C$. This holds for every natural index $k$. By [F3], $\\epsilon_k>0$ and $\\epsilon_k\\to0$, so stability with $Z_0=Z_1=C$ gives $A\\preceq B$. If $m(C)=0$, nonnegativity implies $C=0$ and the conclusion is the premise itself. Conversely, $A\\preceq B$ gives $A+C\\preceq B+C$ by consistency.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For nonnegative $u,v$, step 1.1 gives $u=k+(v-u)^-$ and $v=k+(v-u)^+$. Cancellation from step 1.2 shows $u\\preceq v$ exactly when $(v-u)^-\\preceq(v-u)^+$, which is $u\\preceq_s v$. For general signed maps, adding a common map leaves $v-u$ unchanged, so it leaves signed comparison unchanged.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $u\\preceq_s v$ and $v\\preceq_s w$, then consistency combines $(v-u)^-\\preceq(v-u)^+$ and $(w-v)^-\\preceq(w-v)^+$ into a comparison between the sums of their negative and positive parts. The two sums have a common pointwise minimum; cancel it by step 1.2. The remaining maps are precisely $(w-u)^-$ and $(w-u)^+$, so $u\\preceq_s w$. The same sum-and-cancel argument applied to two independent signed comparisons proves closure under their addition; reflexivity follows from $0\\preceq0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "A1",
            "F2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 separates the zero-mass spectator."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 proves a zero-mass nonnegative spectator is the zero compound."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "one: No one-element boundary changes the result beyond the stated hypotheses.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Under [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]], for equal-mass compounds $A,B,C$, $A+C\\preceq B+C$ if and only if $A\\preceq B$. The signed relation of [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]] is well-defined; on nonnegative equal-mass maps it agrees with $\\preceq$, is invariant under adding the same finite-support signed map to both sides, and is transitive and closed under adding two comparisons.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "03d3a800c27159b8f90de34633e7a01e3f9bcd7294edf5a86aaeba4b4ef12199",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
