          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-signed-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. These signed maps are formal comparison notation, not compounds. Put $d:=v-u$ and define $d^+(x):=\\max\\{d(x),0\\}$ and $d^-(x):=\\max\\{-d(x),0\\}$. Then $d^+,d^-$ are nonnegative finite-support compounds of equal mass, as verified in [[lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation]]. Define\n\n$$u\\preceq_s v\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d^-\\preceq d^+,$$\n\nusing the compound comparison relation of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]]. Thus positive coefficients of $v-u$ remain on the right and negative coefficients move to the left. Signed notation is never a claim that a negative amount of a physical system exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]], for equal-mass compounds $A,B,C$, $A+C\\preceq B+C$ if and only if $A\\preceq B$. The signed relation of [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]] is well-defined; on nonnegative equal-mass maps it agrees with $\\preceq$, is invariant under adding the same finite-support signed map to both sides, and is transitive and closed under adding two comparisons.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $\\delta:=\\mu-\\lambda$. The coefficient difference is $R_\\mu-R_\\lambda=\\delta(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})$ and has total zero. If $\\delta>0$, its negative and positive parts are $\\delta e_{x_0}$ and $\\delta e_{x_1}$; if $\\delta<0$, they are $(-\\delta)e_{x_1}$ and $(-\\delta)e_{x_0}$; if $\\delta=0$, both are zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "If $\\delta=0$, reflexivity gives $R_\\lambda\\preceq_sR_\\mu$. If $\\delta>0$, [F1] reduces this comparison to $\\delta e_{x_0}\\preceq\\delta e_{x_1}$, which follows by positive scaling of $x_0\\preceq x_1$ by $\\delta$. Hence $\\lambda<\\mu$ implies $R_\\lambda\\preceq_sR_\\mu$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If $\\delta<0$ and $R_\\lambda\\preceq_sR_\\mu$, [F1] gives $(-\\delta)e_{x_1}\\preceq(-\\delta)e_{x_0}$. Positive scaling by $1/(-\\delta)$ would imply $x_1\\preceq x_0$, contrary to [A1]. Thus this comparison fails when $\\lambda>\\mu$. The three cases prove the equivalence.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $x_0,x_1\\in X$ satisfy $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$. Boundary empty: No empty family is quantified in the Statement; the input domain is fixed there.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $x_0,x_1\\in X$ satisfy $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$. For every real $\\lambda$, define the signed unit-mass reference map $R_\\lambda:=(1-\\lambda)e_{x_0}+\\lambda e_{x_1}$. For all $\\lambda,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$R_\\lambda\\preceq_s R_\\mu\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\lambda\\le\\mu.$$\n\nThe parameters need not lie in $[0,1]$; outside that interval $R_\\lambda$ is signed notation, not a compound.",
