    "lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a nonempty set $X$ and the compounds of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. A **comparison relation** $\\preceq$ is defined only for pairs $(A,B)$ with $m(A)=m(B)$. Write $A\\sim B$ when both $A\\preceq B$ and $B\\preceq A$; this is equivalence, not equality of coefficient functions. The following are explicit hypotheses on $\\preceq$:\n\n1. **Preorder:** $A\\preceq A$, and $A\\preceq B\\preceq C$ implies $A\\preceq C$.\n2. **Composition consistency:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $A+C\\preceq B+C$ for every compound $C$.\n3. **Positive scaling:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $tA\\preceq tB$ for every real $t>0$.\n4. **Splitting and recombination:** for $x\\in X$, $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, the one-state compound $te_x$ is equivalent to $se_x+(t-s)e_x$.\n5. **Stability:** for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive compounds $Z_0,Z_1$ with $m(Z_0)=m(Z_1)$, and a sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to $0$, if $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\preceq B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\preceq B$.\n6. **Comparison hypothesis (CH):** for every equal-mass pair $A,B$, either $A\\preceq B$ or $B\\preceq A$ (or both).\n\nThese are mathematical assumptions, not conclusions about a physical system. No antisymmetry, continuity, convexity, or strict comparison is included. Strict comparison means $A\\preceq B$ and not $B\\preceq A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set of state labels. A **tagged scalable compound over $X$** is a function $c:X\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}(c):=\\{x\\in X:c(x)\\ne0\\}$ is finite. The coefficient is a dimensionless formal amount; state labels retain their tags, so equal coefficients on different labels do not identify those labels. Compounds are equal exactly when their coefficient functions are equal.\n\nFor compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, define composition and nonnegative scaling pointwise by $(c+d)(x):=c(x)+d(x)$ and $(tc)(x):=t c(x)$. The zero function is the empty compound; in particular $0c=0$. Comparison axioms involving scaling still require a strictly positive scalar unless explicitly stated otherwise. The **mass** of $c$ is the finite sum $m(c):=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)$. It is dimensionless bookkeeping mass, not thermodynamic mass or energy. The compound is positive when $m(c)>0$.\n\nFor every state label $x\\in X$, the **unit-state compound** $e_x$ is the coefficient function with $e_x(y)=1$ if $y=x$ and $e_x(y)=0$ otherwise. Its support is $\\{x\\}$, so it is a tagged compound. In compound comparisons, a state label $x$ denotes $e_x$. Every compound has the canonical finite decomposition $c=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}c(x)e_x$, with an empty sum when $c=0$; coefficients on this support are strictly positive. The mass-one property $m(e_x)=1$ and the equivalence $m(c)=0\\Longleftrightarrow c=0$ are proved in [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nThe formula for mass is independent of the chosen finite set containing the support, and composition and scaling obey $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]] proves these assertions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every tagged scalable compound $c$, the mass in [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] is independent of the finite summation set used. For any compounds $c,d$ and $t\\ge0$, $m(c+d)=m(c)+m(d)$ and $m(tc)=t m(c)$; $m(0)=0$. For every $x\\in X$, the unit-state compound $e_x$ has $m(e_x)=1$. A compound has zero mass if and only if it is the empty compound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-signed-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. These signed maps are formal comparison notation, not compounds. Put $d:=v-u$ and define $d^+(x):=\\max\\{d(x),0\\}$ and $d^-(x):=\\max\\{-d(x),0\\}$. Then $d^+,d^-$ are nonnegative finite-support compounds of equal mass, as verified in [[lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation]]. Define\n\n$$u\\preceq_s v\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d^-\\preceq d^+,$$\n\nusing the compound comparison relation of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]]. Thus positive coefficients of $v-u$ remain on the right and negative coefficients move to the left. Signed notation is never a claim that a negative amount of a physical system exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]], for equal-mass compounds $A,B,C$, $A+C\\preceq B+C$ if and only if $A\\preceq B$. The signed relation of [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]] is well-defined; on nonnegative equal-mass maps it agrees with $\\preceq$, is invariant under adding the same finite-support signed map to both sides, and is transitive and closed under adding two comparisons.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-reference-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $x_0,x_1\\in X$ satisfy $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$. For every real $\\lambda$, define the signed unit-mass reference map $R_\\lambda:=(1-\\lambda)e_{x_0}+\\lambda e_{x_1}$. For all $\\lambda,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$R_\\lambda\\preceq_s R_\\mu\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\lambda\\le\\mu.$$\n\nThe parameters need not lie in $[0,1]$; outside that interval $R_\\lambda$ is signed notation, not a compound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-reciprocal-naturals-tend-zero",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$, the sequence $\\epsilon_n:=1/(n+1)$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, is positive, strictly decreasing, and converges to zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Suppose no positive integer $n$ satisfies $(n+1)e_{x_0}\\preceq ne_{x_1}+e_x$. Both sides have mass $n+1$, so CH gives $ne_{x_1}+e_x\\preceq(n+1)e_{x_0}$. Scale by $1/n$ to obtain $e_{x_1}+n^{-1}e_x\\preceq e_{x_0}+n^{-1}e_{x_0}$. Apply stability to the sequence $\\epsilon_n=1/n$, with bases $e_{x_1},e_{x_0}$ and spectators $e_x,e_{x_0}$, each of mass one. It yields $x_1\\preceq x_0$, contradicting strictness. Thus some $n$ has $(n+1)e_{x_0}\\preceq ne_{x_1}+e_x$, which by [F2] is $R_{-n}\\preceq_s e_x$; hence $-n\\in L_x$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Suppose $L_x$ is unbounded above. For each positive integer $n$, unboundedness supplies $\\lambda\\in L_x$ with $\\lambda>n$; [F3] gives $R_n\\preceq_sR_\\lambda$, and transitivity gives $n\\in L_x$. By [F2], $R_n\\preceq_s e_x$ is the compound comparison $e_{x_0}+n e_{x_1}\\preceq e_x+n e_{x_0}$. Scaling by $1/n$ gives $e_{x_1}+n^{-1}e_{x_0}\\preceq e_{x_0}+n^{-1}e_x$. Stability with bases $e_{x_1},e_{x_0}$ and spectators $e_{x_0},e_x$ now yields $x_1\\preceq x_0$, again a contradiction. Therefore $L_x$ is bounded above.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If $\\lambda\\in L_x$ and $\\mu\\le\\lambda$, [F3] gives $R_\\mu\\preceq_sR_\\lambda$; signed transitivity and $R_\\lambda\\preceq_s e_x$ imply $\\mu\\in L_x$. Thus $L_x$ is downward closed. By steps 1.1 and 1.2 it is nonempty and bounded above, so [F5] supplies the real number $s:=\\sup L_x$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For each $n$, $s-1/n$ is not an upper bound of $L_x$, so some $\\lambda\\in L_x$ has $\\lambda>s-1/n$. Downward closure gives $s-1/n\\in L_x$. Consistency then gives $R_s+n^{-1}e_{x_0}\\preceq_s e_x+n^{-1}e_{x_1}$, because $R_{s-1/n}+n^{-1}e_{x_1}=R_s+n^{-1}e_{x_0}$. Let $K:=R_s^-$. Adding the fixed nonnegative map $K$ to both sides converts this signed comparison into the compound comparisons $R_s^++n^{-1}e_{x_0}\\preceq e_x+K+n^{-1}e_{x_1}$. Their bases have equal mass because $m(R_s^+)-m(K)=1$. Stability with spectators $e_{x_0},e_{x_1}$ yields $R_s^+\\preceq e_x+K$, that is, $R_s\\preceq_s e_x$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Since $s$ is an upper bound, $s+1/n\\notin L_x$. CH therefore gives $e_x\\preceq_s R_{s+1/n}$. Add $n^{-1}e_{x_0}$ to both sides and use $R_{s+1/n}+n^{-1}e_{x_0}=R_s+n^{-1}e_{x_1}$. Adding $K=R_s^-$ converts the result to equal-mass compounds $e_x+K+n^{-1}e_{x_0}\\preceq R_s^++n^{-1}e_{x_1}$. Stability with the same positive unit-mass spectators yields $e_x+K\\preceq R_s^+$, or $e_x\\preceq_sR_s$. Together with step 3.1 this proves $R_s\\sim_s e_x$ and the claimed attainment.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "3.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 proves L_x nonempty."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 and 1.2 use unit-mass state compounds."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Assume [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] on compounds over a nonempty state set $X$, a strict reference pair $x_0\\preceq x_1$ with not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, and the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. Boundary one: No one-element boundary changes the result beyond the stated hypotheses.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] on compounds over a nonempty state set $X$, a strict reference pair $x_0\\preceq x_1$ with not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, and the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. For $x\\in X$ define\n\n$$L_x:=\\{\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R:R_\\lambda\\preceq_s e_x\\}.$$\n\nThen $L_x$ is nonempty, bounded above, and downward closed. Its supremum $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ is a real number and satisfies $R_{S(x)}\\sim_s e_x$. Stability is used to prove attainment; no continuity of states or order is assumed.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "119448b9a7e900ca37ed4e59d53b6770245b2d2732f44d7ff22e33d97ab9b27b",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
