        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "In the strict-reference case, [F3] gives $R_\\lambda\\preceq_s e_{x_0}$ exactly for $\\lambda\\le0$, and $R_\\lambda\\preceq_s e_{x_1}$ exactly for $\\lambda\\le1$. Their suprema are therefore $S(x_0)=0$ and $S(x_1)=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Let $C=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ have mass $M$ and set $q_C:=\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)$. Discard any zero terms in this presentation using [F4]. If the support is empty, $C=0$, $M=q_C=0$ and $C_R=0$, so signed reflexivity gives the claimed equivalence. Otherwise each remaining coefficient is strictly positive. For each remaining constituent, [F2] gives $e_{x_i}\\sim_sR_{S(x_i)}$; positive scaling and finite repeated use of composition combine these into $C\\sim_s C_R$, where $C_R:=\\sum_i a_iR_{S(x_i)}=(M-q_C)e_{x_0}+q_Ce_{x_1}$. This calculation is valid for signed coefficients because the signed relation is additive and translation-invariant by [F1].",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply step 1.2 to both $A$ and $B$, and write $q_A,q_B$ for the resulting scalar sums. Since $A\\sim_sA_R$ and $B\\sim_sB_R$, transitivity gives $A\\preceq B$ exactly when $A_R\\preceq_sB_R$. Their masses agree, and $B_R-A_R=(q_B-q_A)(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})$; [F3] therefore gives $A_R\\preceq_sB_R$ exactly when $q_A\\le q_B$. This proves the representation formula.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "In the no-strict-pair case, take any $x,y\\in X$. By [F4] their unit compounds have equal mass one, so CH gives one of $x\\preceq y$ or $y\\preceq x$; if exactly one held it would be a strict pair, so both hold. Fix $r\\in X$. Let $C$ have mass $M$. If $M=0$, [F4] gives $C=0$, and reflexivity handles this empty compound without applying the positive-scaling axiom at zero. If $M>0$, use the canonical finite decomposition over the support from [F4]; every coefficient there is positive. Scale each constituent equivalence $e_x\\sim e_r$ by its positive coefficient and compose the finitely many comparisons to obtain $C\\sim M e_r$. Thus equal-mass $A,B$ are both empty when their common mass is zero, and otherwise each is equivalent to the same $M e_r$; hence they are equivalent in both cases. For a constant $S(x)=c$, both compound values equal $cM$, including zero for empty compounds, so the representation equivalence holds.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 and 3.1 include zero-mass compounds."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 assigns the empty compound its empty finite sum."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 proves S(x0)=0 and S(x1)=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "3.1 proves the no-strict-pair branch separately."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 checks both references; 1.2 permits values outside [0,1]."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. Boundary nonempty-choice: No arbitrary choice is used; any existence argument is for the fixed finite or individual case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. If there are $x_0,x_1\\in X$ with $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, define $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ using [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. Then $S:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is normalized by $S(x_0)=0$, $S(x_1)=1$, and for all equal-mass compounds $A=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ and $B=\\sum_j b_j e_{y_j}$,\n\n$$A\\preceq B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)\\le\\sum_j b_jS(y_j).$$\n\nThus $S$ is an additive extensive representation on finite compounds. If no strict pair exists, CH instead makes every equal-mass pair of compounds equivalent, and any constant state function gives a (degenerate) additive representation. No concavity, continuity, or physical law follows without additional hypotheses.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "715941f3af23dde78fd6c06d98d70ec040bc667c8625b2980a248c39476c5e69",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "2.1 proves comparison implies value inequality."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "2.1 proves value inequality implies comparison."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "beta-author",
        "notes": "CH and a strict reference pair are explicit; the degenerate branch is separate and no concavity is inferred. Authoring self-check only; independent review remains pending."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-affine-entropy-uniqueness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. If there are $x_0,x_1\\in X$ with $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, define $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ using [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. Then $S:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is normalized by $S(x_0)=0$, $S(x_1)=1$, and for all equal-mass compounds $A=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ and $B=\\sum_j b_j e_{y_j}$,\n\n$$A\\preceq B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)\\le\\sum_j b_jS(y_j).$$\n\nThus $S$ is an additive extensive representation on finite compounds. If no strict pair exists, CH instead makes every equal-mass pair of compounds equivalent, and any constant state function gives a (degenerate) additive representation. No concavity, continuity, or physical law follows without additional hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-signed-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $u,v:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-support real coefficient maps with equal total coefficient $\\sum_xu(x)=\\sum_xv(x)$. These signed maps are formal comparison notation, not compounds. Put $d:=v-u$ and define $d^+(x):=\\max\\{d(x),0\\}$ and $d^-(x):=\\max\\{-d(x),0\\}$. Then $d^+,d^-$ are nonnegative finite-support compounds of equal mass, as verified in [[lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation]]. Define\n\n$$u\\preceq_s v\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d^-\\preceq d^+,$$\n\nusing the compound comparison relation of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]]. Thus positive coefficients of $v-u$ remain on the right and negative coefficients move to the left. Signed notation is never a claim that a negative amount of a physical system exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-spectator-cancellation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]], for equal-mass compounds $A,B,C$, $A+C\\preceq B+C$ if and only if $A\\preceq B$. The signed relation of [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]] is well-defined; on nonnegative equal-mass maps it agrees with $\\preceq$, is invariant under adding the same finite-support signed map to both sides, and is transitive and closed under adding two comparisons.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a nonempty set $X$ and the compounds of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. A **comparison relation** $\\preceq$ is defined only for pairs $(A,B)$ with $m(A)=m(B)$. Write $A\\sim B$ when both $A\\preceq B$ and $B\\preceq A$; this is equivalence, not equality of coefficient functions. The following are explicit hypotheses on $\\preceq$:\n\n1. **Preorder:** $A\\preceq A$, and $A\\preceq B\\preceq C$ implies $A\\preceq C$.\n2. **Composition consistency:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $A+C\\preceq B+C$ for every compound $C$.\n3. **Positive scaling:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $tA\\preceq tB$ for every real $t>0$.\n4. **Splitting and recombination:** for $x\\in X$, $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, the one-state compound $te_x$ is equivalent to $se_x+(t-s)e_x$.\n5. **Stability:** for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive compounds $Z_0,Z_1$ with $m(Z_0)=m(Z_1)$, and a sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to $0$, if $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\preceq B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\preceq B$.\n6. **Comparison hypothesis (CH):** for every equal-mass pair $A,B$, either $A\\preceq B$ or $B\\preceq A$ (or both).\n\nThese are mathematical assumptions, not conclusions about a physical system. No antisymmetry, continuity, convexity, or strict comparison is included. Strict comparison means $A\\preceq B$ and not $B\\preceq A$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix $x\\in X$ and put $s=S(x)$. The equivalence $x\\sim_sR_s$ in [F1] says that the positive and negative parts of their coefficient difference compare in both directions. Since $T$ represents the same order, their finite $T$-sums are equal. By its definition on finite compounds and linearity of finite sums, the signed value of $R_s$ is $(1-s)T(x_0)+sT(x_1)$, including when $s\\notin[0,1]$. Thus $T(x)=T(x_0)+[T(x_1)-T(x_0)]S(x)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Because $x_0\\preceq x_1$ but not conversely, the exact representation property of $T$ gives $T(x_0)<T(x_1)$. Hence $a:=T(x_1)-T(x_0)>0$ and $b:=T(x_0)$ give the asserted form. Conversely, multiplying both sides of any represented-value inequality by $a>0$ and adding the same $b$ times the common compound mass preserves and reflects its order; therefore $aS+b$ represents the same comparisons. The parameters are unique by evaluating at $x_0$ and $x_1$, where $S$ equals $0$ and $1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Assume the strict-reference hypotheses and let $S$ be the normalized representation from [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]]. Boundary empty: No empty family is quantified in the Statement; the input domain is fixed there.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the strict-reference hypotheses and let $S$ be the normalized representation from [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]]. Let $T:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be another additive extensive representation of the same comparisons: for every finite compound $C=\\sum_i c_i e_{x_i}$ define $T(C):=\\sum_i c_iT(x_i)$, and require $C\\preceq D$ iff $T(C)\\le T(D)$ whenever $m(C)=m(D)$. Then there are unique $a>0$ and $b\\in\\mathbb R$ such that $T(x)=aS(x)+b$ for every $x\\in X$. Conversely every such positive affine change represents the same comparisons.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f85571fbdf31434b9514831201f08a7e326e7e9df9963092137f2849e151a898",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
