    "thm-tdmath-common-meter-calibration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. If there are $x_0,x_1\\in X$ with $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, define $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ using [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. Then $S:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is normalized by $S(x_0)=0$, $S(x_1)=1$, and for all equal-mass compounds $A=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ and $B=\\sum_j b_j e_{y_j}$,\n\n$$A\\preceq B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)\\le\\sum_j b_jS(y_j).$$\n\nThus $S$ is an additive extensive representation on finite compounds. If no strict pair exists, CH instead makes every equal-mass pair of compounds equivalent, and any constant state function gives a (degenerate) additive representation. No concavity, continuity, or physical law follows without additional hypotheses.",
