        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a nonempty set $X$ and the compounds of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. A **comparison relation** $\\preceq$ is defined only for pairs $(A,B)$ with $m(A)=m(B)$. Write $A\\sim B$ when both $A\\preceq B$ and $B\\preceq A$; this is equivalence, not equality of coefficient functions. The following are explicit hypotheses on $\\preceq$:\n\n1. **Preorder:** $A\\preceq A$, and $A\\preceq B\\preceq C$ implies $A\\preceq C$.\n2. **Composition consistency:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $A+C\\preceq B+C$ for every compound $C$.\n3. **Positive scaling:** $A\\preceq B$ implies $tA\\preceq tB$ for every real $t>0$.\n4. **Splitting and recombination:** for $x\\in X$, $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, the one-state compound $te_x$ is equivalent to $se_x+(t-s)e_x$.\n5. **Stability:** for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive compounds $Z_0,Z_1$ with $m(Z_0)=m(Z_1)$, and a sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to $0$, if $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\preceq B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\preceq B$.\n6. **Comparison hypothesis (CH):** for every equal-mass pair $A,B$, either $A\\preceq B$ or $B\\preceq A$ (or both).\n\nThese are mathematical assumptions, not conclusions about a physical system. No antisymmetry, continuity, convexity, or strict comparison is included. Strict comparison means $A\\preceq B$ and not $B\\preceq A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-reference-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $x_0,x_1\\in X$ satisfy $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$. For every real $\\lambda$, define the signed unit-mass reference map $R_\\lambda:=(1-\\lambda)e_{x_0}+\\lambda e_{x_1}$. For all $\\lambda,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$R_\\lambda\\preceq_s R_\\mu\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\lambda\\le\\mu.$$\n\nThe parameters need not lie in $[0,1]$; outside that interval $R_\\lambda$ is signed notation, not a compound.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each constituent $(i,x_{ik},z_0)$ of $C$, its mass-two shared compound $u_{i,x_{ik}}$ is equivalent by the exact substitution in [A1] to $e_{r_i}+(1-\\sigma_i(x_{ik}))e_{z_0}+\\sigma_i(x_{ik})e_{z_1}$. Each side has total coefficient two, so this signed substitution is defined. Scale by $t_{ik}>0$ and combine the finitely many comparisons using [A1] and [F2]. The resulting reference-system part is $\\sum_iT_i e_{r_i}$, independent of the chosen states. The meter part has coefficients $T-\\Sigma(C)$ on $e_{z_0}$ and $\\Sigma(C)$ on $e_{z_1}$, where $T:=\\sum_iT_i$. Hence $C$ is equivalent to that fixed reference-system compound plus $(T-\\Sigma(C))e_{z_0}+\\Sigma(C)e_{z_1}$, whose total coefficient is $T+T=2T$. If the collection is empty, both expressions are zero and the equivalence is reflexivity, requiring no order scaling at zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The same substitution for $D$ gives the same reference-system compound because its type totals are the same, and the same total meter mass $T$. Cancel the common reference-system terms by [A1] and [F2]. If $T>0$, the remaining meter expressions have total coefficient $T$; positive scaling by $1/T$ turns them into the unit reference maps $R_{\\Sigma(C)/T}$ and $R_{\\Sigma(D)/T}$. By [F3] these compare exactly when $\\Sigma(C)/T\\le\\Sigma(D)/T$, which is equivalent to $\\Sigma(C)\\le\\Sigma(D)$. Scaling back by $T>0$ proves the reverse comparison equivalence, so no unit-mass reference theorem is applied directly at mass $T$. Reversing the same equivalences proves the converse implication as well. Since $C=C_0+T e_{z_0}$ and $D=D_0+T e_{z_0}$, [F2] also gives $C\\preceq D$ exactly when $C_0\\preceq D_0$; for $T=0$ all these compounds are empty and the criterion is $0\\le0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
