          "reason": "Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set of system types, $X_i$ a nonempty state set with chosen reference $r_i\\in X_i$ for each $i\\in I$, and $M$ a common meter state set with $z_0,z_1\\in M$. Boundary endpoints: No interval endpoint remains implicit in the Statement.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set of system types, $X_i$ a nonempty state set with chosen reference $r_i\\in X_i$ for each $i\\in I$, and $M$ a common meter state set with $z_0,z_1\\in M$. Assume for every $i$ that the product state set $X_i\\times M$ satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]], with strict reference pair $(r_i,z_0)\\prec\\prec(r_i,z_1)$. Let $S_i$ be its normalized representation and put $\\sigma_i(x):=S_i(x,z_0)$. In that product comparison, the unit product-state point mass satisfies $e_{(x,z_0)}\\sim_s(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{(r_i,z_0)}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{(r_i,z_1)}$, by [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. These product-state point masses are distinct from the shared state-and-meter compounds defined next.\n\nAssume in addition a **common-meter compatibility interface**. Let $\\mathcal Y$ contain disjoint families of raw state tags $a_{i,x}$, raw type-reference tags $\\rho_i$, and two distinct raw meter tags $\\zeta_0,\\zeta_1$. Using the unit point masses on these tags, write $e_{r_i}:=e_{\\rho_i}$ and $e_{z_j}:=e_{\\zeta_j}$, and define $u_{i,x}:=e_{a_{i,x}}+e_{z_0}$. Thus $u_{i,x}$ is an explicit mass-two state-and-fixed-meter compound; it is not a unit tag. Compounds over $\\mathcal Y$ use the finite-support convention of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]], and their signed comparison is the one in [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]]. Require the exact substitution\n\n$$u_{i,x}\\sim_s e_{r_i}+(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{z_0}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{z_1}\\qquad(i\\in I,\\ x\\in X_i),$$\n\nfinite substitutions to compose, and the fixed reference sum $\\sum_iT_ie_{r_i}$ to cancel when type totals agree. Both sides of this signed substitution have total coefficient two, even when $\\sigma_i(x)$ lies outside $[0,1]$. Require the common meter point masses to satisfy $e_{z_0}\\preceq e_{z_1}$ and not $e_{z_1}\\preceq e_{z_0}$. These are hypotheses on the comparison algebra, not consequences of separate numerical meter readings.\n\nFor positive coefficients $t_{ik}$ on a finite collection of type-$i$ states $x_{ik}$, define $T_i:=\\sum_k t_{ik}$ and $T:=\\sum_iT_i$. Put $C_0:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}e_{a_{i,x_{ik}}}$ and $C:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}u_{i,x_{ik}}=C_0+T e_{z_0}$. Thus $C_0$ is the state compound of mass $T$ and $C$ is its fixed-meter encoding of mass $2T$. Empty collections give $T=0$ and $C_0=C=0$. Define\n\n$$\\Sigma(C):=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_k t_{ik}\\sigma_i(x_{ik}).$$\n\nFor two such compounds $C,D$ with the same type-total vector $(T_i)_{i\\in I}$, the common comparison satisfies $C\\preceq D$ exactly when $\\Sigma(C)\\le\\Sigma(D)$. Equivalently, the same order criterion holds for the state compounds $C_0,D_0$ after canceling their common $T e_{z_0}$. The index family is finite; no arbitrary-index choice or infinite decomposition is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "6c04bec3747fc2e501b11c1c57fe7e6ebdbba977d1fcc5f8a79c57b520d1665f",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and 1.1\u20132.1: finite references are given and no arbitrary-index selection is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "2.1 proves the forward calibrated comparison."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "2.1 reverses the substitutions for the converse."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "beta-author",
        "notes": "Cross-type common-meter compatibility is explicit; only finitely many types are covered. Authoring self-check only; independent review remains pending."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-vector-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]), with additive identity $0_F$, multiplicative\nidentity $1_F$, and the field axioms as stated there. A **vector space over $F$**,\nalso called an **$F$-vector space**, consists of",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of reals, written $a_k$ for\n$a(k)$. **Finite sums and finite products of $a$ are defined by recursion on the\nupper index**, which is legitimate because of the recursion theorem\n([[thm-recursion]]). That theorem produces a function of one variable, so the\nrunning index has to be carried along inside the value: applying it to the set\n$A = \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$, the starting element $(0, 0)$ and the function\n$f(n, s) = (\\sigma(n),\\, s + a_n)$ gives a unique\n$g : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-finite-sum-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. **Additivity.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n}(a_k + b_k) = \\sum_{k<n} a_k + \\sum_{k<n} b_k$.\n2. **Scaling.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda a_k = \\lambda \\sum_{k<n} a_k$; in particular $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda = n\\lambda$, where $n$ denotes the canonical field image $n\\cdot1_{\\mathbb{R}}$ of the natural n ([[lem-of-q-embeds]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-function-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A natural number is a von Neumann natural ([[def-natural-numbers]]), that is a\nset, and $n = \\{\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N} : m < n \\,\\} = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$\n([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]). Taking $X = n$ therefore gives\n\n$$F^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to F \\,\\},$$\n\nwhose elements are written $x = (x_0, x_1, \\dots, x_{n-1})$ with $x_i := x(i)$ for\n$i < n$. **The coordinates are indexed from $0$**, because $0 \\in n$ whenever\n$n \\ne 0$ and $n \\notin n$ always. The operations read\n\n$$(x + y)_i = x_i + y_i, \\qquad (\\lambda x)_i = \\lambda x_i \\qquad (i < n),$$\n\nand the zero of $F^{n}$ is the tuple all of whose coordinates are $0_F$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-finite-sum-reindexing-and-fubini",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. If $h:T\\to S$ is a bijection and $a:S\\to M$, then $\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{h(t)}=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s$.\n2. If $S$ and $T$ are disjoint and $a:S\\cup T\\to M$, then $\\sum_{u\\in S\\cup T}a_u=\\sum_{s\\in S}a_s+\\sum_{t\\in T}a_t$.\n3. If $a:S\\times T\\to M$, then\n   $$\\sum_{(s,t)\\in S\\times T}a_{s,t}=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\left(\\sum_{t\\in T}a_{s,t}\\right)=\\sum_{t\\in T}\\left(\\sum_{s\\in S}a_{s,t}\\right).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $b$ solves the system and $\\lambda_j\\ge0$ with $\\sum_j\\lambda_jc_j=0$, multiply row $j$ by $\\lambda_j$ and add the finitely many inequalities. The left side is $(\\sum_j\\lambda_jc_j)\\cdot b=0$ by [F2]; hence $0\\ge\\sum_j\\lambda_jh_j$. This proves necessity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For sufficiency, prove by induction on $n$ the alternative: if the finite system has no solution, there is a nonnegative combination of its rows whose coefficient vector is zero and whose right side is positive. If $n=0$, every row has zero left side; infeasibility means some $h_j>0$, and that single row is the certificate. If $m=0$, the system is feasible, so this case cannot occur under the induction claim's antecedent.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Let $n>0$ and assume the alternative for $n-1$ variables. Write $c_j=(a_j,\\alpha_j)$ with $a_j\\in\\mathbb R^{n-1}$ and split the row indices into $P=\\{j:\\alpha_j>0\\}$, $N=\\{j:\\alpha_j<0\\}$, and $Z=\\{j:\\alpha_j=0\\}$. Retain each row $(a_j,h_j)$ for $j\\in Z$. For every $p\\in P$ and $q\\in N$, add the reduced row $((-\\alpha_q)a_p+\\alpha_p a_q,\\;(-\\alpha_q)h_p+\\alpha_p h_q)$. Every multiplier used is positive.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "The reduced system in $u\\in\\mathbb R^{n-1}$ is feasible exactly when the original is. Indeed, a solution $(u,t)$ implies every retained inequality; adding row $p$ multiplied by $-\\alpha_q>0$ to row $q$ multiplied by $\\alpha_p>0$ cancels $t$ and gives each pair row. Conversely, if $u$ satisfies the reduced system, rows in $P$ impose finitely many lower bounds $t\\ge(h_p-a_p\\cdot u)/\\alpha_p$, and rows in $N$ impose finitely many upper bounds $t\\le(h_q-a_q\\cdot u)/\\alpha_q$. Each pair row is exactly the inequality that its lower bound is no greater than its upper bound. If both sets are nonempty, their finite maximum lower bound is at most their finite minimum upper bound, so choose $t$ between them. If only lower bounds occur choose their maximum; if only upper bounds occur choose their minimum; if neither occurs choose $t=0$. Thus the original system has a solution.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If the original system is infeasible, step 1.4 makes the reduced system infeasible. By induction there are nonnegative multipliers on reduced rows with zero combined first $n-1$ coordinates and positive combined right side. Lift each retained zero-row multiplier to its original row; lift each pair-row multiplier $\\mu_{pq}$ to coefficients $-\\alpha_q\\mu_{pq}$ on row $p$ and $\\alpha_p\\mu_{pq}$ on row $q$. All lifted coefficients are nonnegative. Every pair lift has last coordinate $(-\\alpha_q)\\alpha_p+\\alpha_p\\alpha_q=0$; the total first coordinates vanish by the reduced certificate, and its right side remains positive. This is the required certificate for the original system and completes the induction.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If the stated multiplier condition holds, the certificate from step 2.1 cannot exist, because it would have zero combined left coefficient and positive combined right side. Therefore the system is feasible. Together with step 1.1 this proves both directions, including the empty-variable base case in step 1.2, the zero/positive/negative sign partition and bound reconstruction in steps 1.3\u20131.4, and the lifted certificate in step 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 and 3.1 handle empty rows and zero variables."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 proves the n=0 base case."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$ be finite, let $c_j\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $h_j\\in\\mathbb R$ for $0\\le j<m$. Boundary one: No one-element boundary changes the result beyond the stated hypotheses.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$ be finite, let $c_j\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $h_j\\in\\mathbb R$ for $0\\le j<m$. The finite system\n\n$$c_j\\cdot b\\ge h_j\\qquad(0\\le j<m)$$\n\nhas a solution $b\\in\\mathbb R^n$ if and only if every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^m$ with $\\lambda_j\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{j<m}\\lambda_jc_j=0$ satisfies $\\sum_{j<m}\\lambda_jh_j\\le0$. Dot products and sums are finite. This includes empty rows, empty variables, and an empty inequality family.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f7cf0650199d0e3c47d312e7bd04d82894efc4ce86a10900375aa11c596b5ba0",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.3 and 1.4 retain zero-coefficient rows and one-sided bound cases."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.4 chooses the finite lower or upper endpoint when only one side occurs."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.4 uses finite maxima and minima only."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 proves feasibility implies the multiplier condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "2.1 and 3.1 prove the converse."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "beta-author",
        "notes": "All signs, zero rows, empty families, one-sided bounds, and certificate lifting are treated. Authoring self-check only; independent review remains pending."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-reaction-offset-feasibility": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-common-meter-calibration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set of system types, $X_i$ a nonempty state set with chosen reference $r_i\\in X_i$ for each $i\\in I$, and $M$ a common meter state set with $z_0,z_1\\in M$. Assume for every $i$ that the product state set $X_i\\times M$ satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]], with strict reference pair $(r_i,z_0)\\prec\\prec(r_i,z_1)$. Let $S_i$ be its normalized representation and put $\\sigma_i(x):=S_i(x,z_0)$. In that product comparison, the unit product-state point mass satisfies $e_{(x,z_0)}\\sim_s(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{(r_i,z_0)}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{(r_i,z_1)}$, by [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. These product-state point masses are distinct from the shared state-and-meter compounds defined next.\n\nAssume in addition a **common-meter compatibility interface**. Let $\\mathcal Y$ contain disjoint families of raw state tags $a_{i,x}$, raw type-reference tags $\\rho_i$, and two distinct raw meter tags $\\zeta_0,\\zeta_1$. Using the unit point masses on these tags, write $e_{r_i}:=e_{\\rho_i}$ and $e_{z_j}:=e_{\\zeta_j}$, and define $u_{i,x}:=e_{a_{i,x}}+e_{z_0}$. Thus $u_{i,x}$ is an explicit mass-two state-and-fixed-meter compound; it is not a unit tag. Compounds over $\\mathcal Y$ use the finite-support convention of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]], and their signed comparison is the one in [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]]. Require the exact substitution\n\n$$u_{i,x}\\sim_s e_{r_i}+(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{z_0}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{z_1}\\qquad(i\\in I,\\ x\\in X_i),$$\n\nfinite substitutions to compose, and the fixed reference sum $\\sum_iT_ie_{r_i}$ to cancel when type totals agree. Both sides of this signed substitution have total coefficient two, even when $\\sigma_i(x)$ lies outside $[0,1]$. Require the common meter point masses to satisfy $e_{z_0}\\preceq e_{z_1}$ and not $e_{z_1}\\preceq e_{z_0}$. These are hypotheses on the comparison algebra, not consequences of separate numerical meter readings.\n\nFor positive coefficients $t_{ik}$ on a finite collection of type-$i$ states $x_{ik}$, define $T_i:=\\sum_k t_{ik}$ and $T:=\\sum_iT_i$. Put $C_0:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}e_{a_{i,x_{ik}}}$ and $C:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}u_{i,x_{ik}}=C_0+T e_{z_0}$. Thus $C_0$ is the state compound of mass $T$ and $C$ is its fixed-meter encoding of mass $2T$. Empty collections give $T=0$ and $C_0=C=0$. Define\n\n$$\\Sigma(C):=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_k t_{ik}\\sigma_i(x_{ik}).$$\n\nFor two such compounds $C,D$ with the same type-total vector $(T_i)_{i\\in I}$, the common comparison satisfies $C\\preceq D$ exactly when $\\Sigma(C)\\le\\Sigma(D)$. Equivalently, the same order criterion holds for the state compounds $C_0,D_0$ after canceling their common $T e_{z_0}$. The index family is finite; no arbitrary-index choice or infinite decomposition is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$ be finite, let $c_j\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $h_j\\in\\mathbb R$ for $0\\le j<m$. The finite system\n\n$$c_j\\cdot b\\ge h_j\\qquad(0\\le j<m)$$\n\nhas a solution $b\\in\\mathbb R^n$ if and only if every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^m$ with $\\lambda_j\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{j<m}\\lambda_jc_j=0$ satisfies $\\sum_{j<m}\\lambda_jh_j\\le0$. Dot products and sums are finite. This includes empty rows, empty variables, and an empty inequality family.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to the rows $c_a=\\nu_a$ and $h_a=-d_a$. The row system $\\nu_a\\cdot b\\ge-d_a$ is exactly the required offset system. Its multiplier condition is $\\sum_a\\lambda_a\\nu_a=0\\Rightarrow\\sum_a\\lambda_a(-d_a)\\le0$, which is equivalent to $\\sum_a\\lambda_ad_a\\ge0$. This proves the if-and-only-if, including $A=\\varnothing$ where $b=0$ is feasible and the multiplier condition is vacuous. If $r=0$ and $A$ is nonempty, every $\\nu_a=0$; testing the coordinate multiplier for each channel makes the criterion equivalent to $d_a\\ge0$ for every $a$, and then the unique empty vector $b$ satisfies all rows.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For any channel with actual change $\\Delta\\sigma_a\\ge d_a$, add $b\\cdot\\nu_a$ to both sides of that bound and use the solved row inequality $b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge-d_a$. Then $\\Delta\\sigma_a+b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge d_a+b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge0$. This is a sufficient monotonicity conclusion for the stated channels.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 handles A empty, when b=0 is feasible."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1: when r=0, each zero vector condition reduces to d_a>=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $A$ be a finite set of channel labels and $r\\in\\mathbb N$. For each $a\\in A$, take $\\nu_a\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and $d_a\\in\\mathbb R$ as independently specified real input data; this theorem does not define channel input/output compounds, a calibration map, or a map producing $d_a$. In an application, separate model data may specify vectors $\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}},\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and set $\\nu_a:=\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}-\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}}$, while a separately supplied real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$ may be designated as a channel's calibrated entropy change and $d_a\\le\\Delta\\sigma_a$ as its lower bound. These are additional application premises, not conclusions or inputs derived here. Use the dot product and coordinatewise finite vector sums defined in [[thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative]]: for $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^r$, $u\\cdot v:=\\sum_{k<r}u(k)v(k)$, and $(\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a)(k):=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a(k)$. A multiplier $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ is a function $A\\to\\mathbb R$. There exists an offset vector $b\\in\\mathbb R^r$ satisfying\n\n$$b\\cdot\\nu_a+d_a\\ge0\\qquad(a\\in A)$$\n\nif and only if every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ with $\\lambda_a\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a=0$ satisfies $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_ad_a\\ge0$. If a separate model supplies for each $a$ a real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$, designated as its transition entropy change, and assumes $\\Delta\\sigma_a\\ge d_a$, such a vector guarantees $\\Delta\\sigma_a+b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge0$ for those channels. The criterion is exact for the finite lower-bound inequalities; it does not claim necessity for actual transitions when $d_a$ is only a loose bound.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "039abb7ebee982f60948eb471817f7cc1f90dc4c75fb8aefb0f20437abacd466",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "reason": "The finite alternative already covers a singleton channel family; no separate one-channel normalization or calibration result is asserted. Each nu_a and d_a is independent real input data; actual entropy changes are an additional application premise."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: the multiplier condition includes zero stoichiometric vectors."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $A$ be a finite set of channel labels and $r\\in\\mathbb N$. For each $a\\in A$, take $\\nu_a\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and $d_a\\in\\mathbb R$ as independently specified real input data; this theorem does not define channel input/output compounds, a calibration map, or a map producing $d_a$. In an application, separate model data may specify vectors $\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}},\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and set $\\nu_a:=\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}-\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}}$, while a separately supplied real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$ may be designated as a channel's calibrated entropy change and $d_a\\le\\Delta\\sigma_a$ as its lower bound. These are additional application premises, not conclusions or inputs derived here. Use the dot product and coordinatewise finite vector sums defined in [[thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative]]: for $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^r$, $u\\cdot v:=\\sum_{k<r}u(k)v(k)$, and $(\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a)(k):=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a(k)$. A multiplier $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ is a function $A\\to\\mathbb R$. There exists an offset vector $b\\in\\mathbb R^r$ satisfying\n\n$$b\\cdot\\nu_a+d_a\\ge0\\qquad(a\\in A)$$\n\nif and only if every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ with $\\lambda_a\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a=0$ satisfies $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_ad_a\\ge0$. If a separate model supplies for each $a$ a real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$, designated as its transition entropy change, and assumes $\\Delta\\sigma_a\\ge d_a$, such a vector guarantees $\\Delta\\sigma_a+b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge0$ for those channels. The criterion is exact for the finite lower-bound inequalities; it does not claim necessity for actual transitions when $d_a$ is only a loose bound.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "039abb7ebee982f60948eb471817f7cc1f90dc4c75fb8aefb0f20437abacd466",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "reason": "The exact finite real-vector inequality criterion has no interval endpoint. The conditional actual-transition conclusion retains separately supplied real Delta sigma_a>=d_a and makes no claim when that application premise fails."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $A$ be a finite set of channel labels and $r\\in\\mathbb N$. For each $a\\in A$, take $\\nu_a\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and $d_a\\in\\mathbb R$ as independently specified real input data; this theorem does not define channel input/output compounds, a calibration map, or a map producing $d_a$. In an application, separate model data may specify vectors $\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}},\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and set $\\nu_a:=\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{out}}-\\nu_a^{\\mathrm{in}}$, while a separately supplied real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$ may be designated as a channel's calibrated entropy change and $d_a\\le\\Delta\\sigma_a$ as its lower bound. These are additional application premises, not conclusions or inputs derived here. Use the dot product and coordinatewise finite vector sums defined in [[thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative]]: for $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^r$, $u\\cdot v:=\\sum_{k<r}u(k)v(k)$, and $(\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a)(k):=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a(k)$. A multiplier $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ is a function $A\\to\\mathbb R$. There exists an offset vector $b\\in\\mathbb R^r$ satisfying\n\n$$b\\cdot\\nu_a+d_a\\ge0\\qquad(a\\in A)$$\n\nif and only if every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^A$ with $\\lambda_a\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_a\\nu_a=0$ satisfies $\\sum_{a\\in A}\\lambda_ad_a\\ge0$. If a separate model supplies for each $a$ a real number $\\Delta\\sigma_a$, designated as its transition entropy change, and assumes $\\Delta\\sigma_a\\ge d_a$, such a vector guarantees $\\Delta\\sigma_a+b\\cdot\\nu_a\\ge0$ for those channels. The criterion is exact for the finite lower-bound inequalities; it does not claim necessity for actual transitions when $d_a$ is only a loose bound.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "039abb7ebee982f60948eb471817f7cc1f90dc4c75fb8aefb0f20437abacd466",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "reason": "The only enumeration in proof1.1 is of one specified finite channel-label set; no arbitrary infinite-family choice, calibration map or derived channel entropy data is asserted."
        },
