    "lem-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space-well-defined": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of formal type labels closed under the formal operations $(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\mapsto\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma'$ and $(t,\\Gamma)\\mapsto t\\Gamma$ for every real $t>0$. Let $F:=\\mathbb R^{(\\mathcal G)}$ be the vector space of finite-support real functions on $\\mathcal G$, and let $e_\\Gamma$ be the function equal to $1$ at $\\Gamma$ and $0$ elsewhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-function-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The two boundary cases.** $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, so $n = 0$ is a genuine\ncase. Since $0 = \\varnothing$, the set $F^{0} = F^{\\varnothing}$ has exactly one\nelement, the empty function; that element is $0_{F^{0}}$, so $F^{0}$ is the\n**zero space** $\\{0_{F^{0}}\\}$, not the empty set. For $n = 1$ we have\n$1 = \\{0\\}$, and the map $F^{1} \\to F$ sending $x$ to its single coordinate $x_0$\nis a bijection satisfying $(x+y)_0 = x_0 + y_0$ and $(\\lambda x)_0 = \\lambda x_0$;\nwe use it to read $F^{1}$ as $F$ where convenient. (No general notion of\nisomorphism of vector spaces is available on this page, and none is claimed here:\nwhat is asserted is exactly the displayed bijection and the two displayed\nequations.)",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-vector-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]), with additive identity $0_F$, multiplicative\nidentity $1_F$, and the field axioms as stated there. A **vector space over $F$**,\nalso called an **$F$-vector space**, consists of",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-linear-subspace",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "- **(W1)** $0_V \\in W$;\n- **(W2)** $W$ is closed under the vector addition: $u, v \\in W$ implies\n  $u + v \\in W$;\n- **(W3)** $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication: $\\lambda \\in F$ and\n  $v \\in W$ imply $\\lambda v \\in W$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$\\forall x\\,\\forall y\\,\\exists z\\,\\forall t\\,\\bigl(t \\in z \\leftrightarrow (t = x \\vee t = y)\\bigr)$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-union",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "of the language of set theory ([[def-language-of-set-theory]]): for any set $x$\nthere is a set $y$ whose elements are exactly the sets $z$ belonging to some\nmember $s$ of $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-infinity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$\\exists I\\,\\Bigl(\\exists e\\,\\bigl(e \\in I \\wedge \\neg\\exists t\\,(t \\in e)\\bigr) \\wedge \\forall y\\,\\bigl(y \\in I \\to \\exists s\\,(s \\in I \\wedge \\forall t\\,(t \\in s \\leftrightarrow (t \\in y \\vee t = y)))\\bigr)\\Bigr)$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-schema-of-separation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$\\forall \\bar p\\,\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,\\forall z\\,\\bigl(z \\in y \\leftrightarrow (z \\in x \\wedge \\varphi(z,\\bar p))\\bigr).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-power-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Power Set** is the sentence",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-axiom-schema-of-replacement",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$\\forall \\bar p\\,\\forall A\\,\\Bigl(\\forall z\\,\\bigl(z \\in A \\to \\exists! w\\,\\varphi(z,w,\\bar p)\\bigr) \\to \\exists B\\,\\forall w\\,\\bigl(w \\in B \\leftrightarrow \\exists z\\,(z \\in A \\wedge \\varphi(z,w,\\bar p))\\bigr)\\Bigr).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-cartesian-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $A$ and $B$ be sets. By\n[[lem-ordered-pairs-lie-in-the-double-power-set]] every ordered pair $(a,b)$ with\n$a \\in A$ and $b \\in B$ is an element of $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(A \\cup B))$, so\n[[def-axiom-schema-of-separation]] applied inside that set produces the\n**Cartesian product**",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The set of all functions $\\mathcal G\\to\\mathbb R$ exists and is a vector space by [F2]. Separation [F4] selects the functions whose support is finite, so $F$ is a set. It contains zero; if $c,d\\in F$, then $\\operatorname{supp}(c+d)\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}(c)\\cup\\operatorname{supp}(d)$, a finite union, and $\\operatorname{supp}(tc)\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}(c)$ for every scalar $t$. Thus $F$ is a linear subspace of the function space by [F3], hence a real vector space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The index sets $\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ and $\\mathbb R_{>0}\\times\\mathcal G$ exist by [F4]. Replacement applied to the two displayed relation maps in [F1] gives the set $\\mathcal R$; each relation vector has support of size at most three and lies in $F$. The family of finite subsets of $\\mathcal R$ is a subset of its power set, and the coefficient functions on each such finite subset form sets by [F2] and [F4]. Replacement under finite summation therefore gives the set $N\\subseteq F$ of all finite linear combinations.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The zero vector is in $N$ as the empty sum. If $u,v\\in N$, write each as a finite combination of relation vectors; their sum is the finite combination on the union of those two finite index sets, adding coefficients where an index repeats. For $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $tu$ is obtained by multiplying every coefficient by $t$. Hence $N$ is closed under addition and scalar multiplication and is a linear subspace of $F$ by [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Define $c\\sim d$ on $F$ exactly when $c-d\\in N$. Since $0\\in N$, $N$ is closed under negatives (take scalar $-1$), and under addition, this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive. Each class $[c]=\\{d\\in F:d-c\\in N\\}$ is a subset of $F$ by separation; replacement on the set $F$ gives the quotient set of these classes.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "If $c\\sim c'$ and $d\\sim d'$, then $(c+d)-(c'+d')=(c-c')+(d-d')\\in N$; and for any scalar $t$, $tc-tc'=t(c-c')\\in N$. Therefore $[c]+[d]:=[c+d]$ and $t[c]:=[tc]$ do not depend on representatives. The quotient map $c\\mapsto[c]$ preserves zero, addition and scalar multiplication; applying it to each vector-space identity in $F$ proves all vector-space axioms in $F/N$. Every generator in $\\mathcal R$ lies in $N$, so its class is zero and the two displayed relations hold.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.2 and 2.1 include the empty relation set and empty linear combination."
        },
