    "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-vector-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "- a set $V$, whose elements are called **vectors**;\n- a binary operation $+ : V \\times V \\to V$ on $V$ ([[def-binary-operation]]),\n  the **vector addition**;\n- an element $0_V \\in V$, the **zero vector**;\n- a map $\\cdot \\,:\\, F \\times V \\to V$, the **scalar multiplication**, written\n  $\\lambda v := \\cdot(\\lambda, v)$;",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-linear-subspace",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "So $(W,+,0_V)$ is an abelian group, which is axiom (V1), and $W$ is a vector\nspace over $F$ **whose zero vector and whose additive inverses are those of\n$V$**. In the language of [[def-subgroup]], the three displayed conditions\n(S1) $0_V \\in W$, (S2) closure under addition and (S3) closure under additive\ninverses all hold, so $W$ is a subgroup of the abelian group $(V,+,0_V)$\n([[def-group]]); that reading, and its converse, are recorded as\n[[lem-linear-subspace-is-a-subgroup]] and are cited from there rather than\nre-argued below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-sublinear-functional",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real vector space ([[def-vector-space]]). A function\n$p:X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a **sublinear functional** if",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-hahn-banach-dominated-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be a real vector space, let\n$M \\subseteq X$ be a linear subspace, let\n$p:X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be sublinear, and let $f:M \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be linear with\n$f(m) \\le p(m)$ for every $m \\in M$. Then there exists a linear functional\n$F:X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $F|_M=f$ and $F(x) \\le p(x)$ for every $x \\in X$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-every-vector-space-has-a-basis",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Assume the Axiom of Choice**, through\n[[thm-every-independent-set-extends-to-a-basis]]. Then every vector space $V$\nover a field $F$ ([[def-vector-space]]) has a basis ([[def-linear-basis]]).",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-every-independent-set-extends-to-a-basis",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), which is what\n[[thm-zorn]] is proved from. Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $F$\n([[def-vector-space]]) and let $L \\subseteq S \\subseteq V$ with $L$ linearly\nindependent ([[def-linear-independence]]) and $\\operatorname{span}(S) = V$\n([[def-linear-combination-and-span]]). Then there is a basis $B$ of $V$\n([[def-linear-basis]]) with",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-linear-independence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "- **The empty list is independent.** For $n = 0$ the only list of scalars is the\n  empty one, and the condition \"$\\lambda_i = 0_F$ for every $i < 0$\" holds\n  vacuously.\n- **$\\varnothing \\subseteq V$ is independent.** The only function $v : n \\to\n  \\varnothing$ is the empty one, with $n = 0$, and it is independent by the\n  previous point.\n- **$\\{0_V\\}$ is dependent.** The list $v : 1 \\to \\{0_V\\}$ with $v_0 = 0_V$ is\n  injective, and taking $\\lambda_0 = 1_F$ gives\n  $\\sum_{i<1}\\lambda_i v_i = 0_V + 1_F 0_V = 0_V$ by the recursion of\n  [[def-monoid-finite-product]] and $\\lambda 0_V = 0_V$\n  ([[lem-vector-space-elementary-consequences]]), while $1_F \\ne 0_F$ in a field\n  ([[def-field]]). So $\\{0_V\\}$, and hence every subset of $V$ containing $0_V$,\n  is linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-linear-basis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "- **(B1)** $B$ is linearly independent ([[def-linear-independence]]), and\n- **(B2)** $B$ spans $V$, that is $\\operatorname{span}(B) = V$\n  ([[def-linear-combination-and-span]], which is where the words *spans* and\n  *spanning set* are fixed; they are not redefined here).",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The class of $c\\in F$ is $[c]$. The defining relations are $[e_{\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma'}]=[e_\\Gamma]+[e_{\\Gamma'}]$ and $[e_{t\\Gamma}]=t[e_\\Gamma]$. The set existence, subspace properties, quotient operations and vector-space laws are proved in [[lem-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space-well-defined]]. These are formal algebraic type labels; no actual catalyst-chain cost or physical existence claim is built into $V_{\\mathcal G}$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the construction in [[def-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space]], $F$ and $N$ are sets, $F$ is a real vector space, $N$ is a linear subspace of $F$, and $F/N$ has representative-independent vector operations making it a real vector space. Its displayed product and positive-scaling relations hold in the quotient.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The set $W$ contains $0$ because $p(0)=0$. If $x,y\\in W$, subadditivity gives $p(x+y)\\le p(x)+p(y)<+\\infty$, so $x+y\\in W$. For $t>0$, homogeneity gives $p(tx)=tp(x)<+\\infty$. If $t<0$, no-sinks gives $-x\\in W$, and positive homogeneity applied to $|t|(-x)$ puts $tx$ in $W$; $0x=0\\in W$. Thus $W$ is a linear subspace. The restriction $p|_W$ is finite-valued, positively homogeneous and subadditive, so it is a real-valued sublinear functional.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $M=\\{0\\}\\subseteq W$ and let $f_0(0)=0$. This is linear and satisfies $f_0(0)=0=p(0)$. The real ordered field is complete by [F4], as used in the one-step interval argument of the published theorem. Apply [F3] to $p|_W$ and $f_0$; it gives a linear $\\ell:W\\to\\mathbb R$ with $\\ell(x)\\le p(x)$ for every $x\\in W$. Applying this at $-x\\in W$ yields $-\\ell(x)=\\ell(-x)\\le p(-x)$, so $-p(-x)\\le\\ell(x)\\le p(x)$ on $W$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F5], choose a basis $B_W$ of $W$ and extend its independent set to a basis $B_V$ of $V$. Assign value $\\ell(b)$ to $b\\in B_W$ and $0$ to $b\\in B_V\\setminus B_W$. For any $v\\in V$, [F6] gives a finite basis expression; define $L(v)$ as the same finite linear combination of assigned real values. If two expressions represented $v$, subtracting and collecting equal basis vectors would give a finite zero combination; independence in [F6] forces every collected coefficient to be zero. Thus $L(v)$ is well-defined. Concatenating finite expressions proves additivity and scalar homogeneity, and the basis expression of any $w\\in W$ uses only $B_W$, so $L|_W=\\ell$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $x\\in W$, step 3.1 and step 2.1 give $-p(-x)\\le L(x)\\le p(x)$. If $x\\notin W$, then $p(x)=+\\infty$ by the definition of $W$; no-sinks implies $p(-x)=+\\infty$ as well, so the extended bounds reduce to $-\\infty\\le L(x)\\le+\\infty$. This proves the global sandwich.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the type-offset application, linearity of $L$ and the quotient relations in [F7] give $B(\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma')=B(\\Gamma)+B(\\Gamma')$ and $B(t\\Gamma)=tB(\\Gamma)$. Apply the sandwich to $e_\\Gamma-e_{\\Gamma'}$ and its negative, using the assumed values of $p$, to obtain the two displayed bounds for $B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')$. For an admitted transition, its real baseline change is at least $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, so that cost value must be finite. The corrected change equals $[\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)] + B(\\Gamma')-B(\\Gamma)=[\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)]-L(e_\\Gamma-e_{\\Gamma'})$. The assumed lower bound on the baseline change and the upper sandwich bound $L(e_\\Gamma-e_{\\Gamma'})\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ make this at least $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')-F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=0$. This proves only the stated conditional monotonicity.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "1.1 and 3.1 include W={0} with empty basis."
        },
